/**
  @page examples-pdes-poisson Solving the Poisson equation
  @brief From a boundary-value problem to a finite element solution and visualization.

  This example solves the Poisson equation on the unit square with a constant
  source and zero values on the boundary. It constructs a triangular mesh,
  expresses the weak form in Rodin, solves the resulting linear system, and
  exports the solution for visualization.

  @m_keywords{Poisson}
  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-pdes-poisson-intro The mathematical problem

  Let @f$\Omega = (0,1)^2@f$. The unknown scalar field @f$u@f$ satisfies
  @f[
    \begin{aligned}
      -\Delta u &= 1 &&\text{in }\Omega,\\
      u &= 0 &&\text{on }\partial\Omega.
    \end{aligned}
  @f]
  The source is positive throughout the domain, while the homogeneous Dirichlet
  condition fixes the field to zero along every edge. The resulting solution is
  positive in the interior and vanishes on the boundary.

  To obtain the weak form, multiply the equation by a test function @f$v@f$
  and integrate over the domain. Integration by parts transfers one derivative
  from @f$u@f$ to @f$v@f$; the boundary term vanishes because @f$v@f$ has zero
  trace. The problem becomes: find @f$u\in H^1_0(\Omega)@f$ such that
  @f[
    \int_\Omega \nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,dx = \int_\Omega v\,dx
    \qquad\text{for every }v\in H^1_0(\Omega).
  @f]
  Rodin approximates this problem in a finite-dimensional space of continuous,
  piecewise linear functions.

  @section examples-pdes-poisson-mesh 1. Construct the mesh

  A uniform grid with 16 vertices in each coordinate direction is split into
  triangles. `UniformGrid` uses unit spacing, so the coordinates are scaled by
  @f$1/(16-1)@f$ to obtain the unit square. The resulting mesh has 256 vertices
  and 450 triangles.

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-mesh

  The final line computes edge-to-cell connectivity. This allows Rodin to
  identify the exterior edges on which the boundary condition is imposed.

  @section examples-pdes-poisson-problem 2. Define the finite element problem

  `P1` defines the space of functions that are continuous across the mesh and
  affine on each triangle. The trial function `u` represents the unknown, and
  the test function `v` represents the arbitrary test function in the weak form.

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-form

  The first `Integral` is the stiffness term, corresponding to the left-hand
  side of the weak form. The second is the source term with @f$f=1@f$.
  `DirichletBC(u, Zero())` imposes zero values on the entire exterior boundary.
  Rodin writes the equation as a residual, so the source term is subtracted
  from the stiffness term; setting that residual to zero recovers the weak form.

  @section examples-pdes-poisson-solve 3. Solve the linear system

  After the boundary values are imposed, the Poisson system is symmetric
  positive definite. The conjugate gradient solver is therefore suitable for
  this problem. `solve()` assembles and solves the system, and `success()`
  checks that the solve completed successfully before the result is exported.

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-solve

  The computed finite element field is available through `u.getSolution()`.

  @section examples-pdes-poisson-output 4. Export and inspect the result

  The solution and its mesh are written in XDMF format. The accompanying HDF5
  files hold the numerical data and must remain alongside `Poisson.xdmf`.

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-export

  @m_class{m-flat}
  @image html PDEs/Poisson.png "Computed solution on the unit square. Height and color represent the scalar field." width=360px

  In ParaView:

  1. Open `Poisson.xdmf` and select **Apply**.
  2. Color the mesh by the point field **u**.
  3. Apply **Warp By Scalar** using **u** to display the field as a surface.
  4. Select **Surface With Edges** to make the triangulation visible.

  On this mesh, the computed nodal values range from @f$0@f$ on the boundary to
  approximately @f$0.072862@f$ in the interior. The surface shown above uses
  the same solution; ParaView amplifies its height by a factor of eight for visibility.

  @section examples-pdes-poisson-full Complete program

  The complete source combines the steps above and can be built with the other
  Rodin examples. The output files are written to the working directory.

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-source

  See @ref PDEs/Poisson.cpp "Poisson.cpp" for the source, the
  @ref guides-getting-started-installation "installation guide" for build setup,
  and the @ref guides-io "I/O guide" for additional export options.

  @example PDEs/Poisson.cpp @m_examplenavigation{examples-pdes-poisson,PDEs/} @m_footernavigation
*/
